        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\alpha=\\frac\\pi2$.",
          "worked": "The complementary-angle identity is $\\sin x=\\cos\\left(\\frac\\pi2-x\\right)$, so one possible value is $\\alpha=\\frac\\pi2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0,
        "responseSpace": "scaffold"
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-p1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, solve $\\cos\\left(\\frac\\pi{12}+x\\right)=\\sin x$ in the domain $x\\in\\left[0,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\frac{5\\pi}{24}$.",
          "worked": "Using part (a),\n$$\\cos\\left(\\frac\\pi{12}+x\\right)=\\cos\\left(\\frac\\pi2-x\\right).$$\nBoth angles lie in $[0,\\pi]$, where cosine is strictly decreasing, so the angles are equal.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac\\pi{12}+x&=\\frac\\pi2-x\\\\2x&=\\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\\\x&=\\frac{5\\pi}{24}.\\end{align*}$$\nThis lies in the given domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      }
    ]
